Subject: [#94-2-104] SCI: Brain Wave Alteration

Kennita asks:

> have you had personal experience with ... machines that help one alter one's
> consciousness by ... goggles and a sound generator [?]

I tried one once -- I don't remember the brand name, but its interface to the
sensory apparatus consisted of a sleep-mask style goggle with an LED in front
of each eye, inside it, and earphones.  The lights flashed and the earphones
produced (I think) clicks (it has been several years).  These were connected
to a programmable driver which would vary frequencies in various ways.  It
had several built-in programs.  What I did was to try one of these.  I had
no objective way of monitoring my brain waves or any of the other things that
might be of interest as results, but I did have the subjective impression that
the device affected my mood and level of alertness.  I was intrigued enough
to write the company that made them for a flyer and price list (name forgotten,
flyer since discarded) but not enough to buy one.  I believe I recall the
price was several hundred dollars.  Likely physical and electronic complexity
of the device was comparable to a "walkman" or to a simple calculator;
prices would likely have been lower if there had been more competition and/or
more volume.

I regarded the device as fun and interesting.  If I had had the opportunity
I would have tried it again.  I might have bought one on speculation at the
$50 price level, but not at any price much higher.

