From: Christopher Michael Rasch <rasch@leland.Stanford.EDU>
Date: Thu, 10 Feb 1994 21:55:32 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-2-107] How I found out about Extropians.

Hi all--

This post is in answer to the question,

"How did you find out about the Extropians?"  

	I read "The Fountainhead" when I was a junior in high school.  While
attending a summer camp in 1990, I attended a lecture given by Hans Moravec.
He gave a summary of his book "Mind Children."  I found his ideas profoundly
exciting.
	During my freshman and sophomore year in college, I read David
Friedman's "Machinery of Freedom" and Drexler's "Engines of Creation."
While reading alt.society.anarchy, I read a post by Perry Metzger which
referred to the extropians mailing list; he forwarded the address to me.


	I recently discovered two books which may be of interest to other 
Extropians.  The first is "How Children Fail" by John Holt.  Holt was a school
teacher at a private grade school.  It's a collection of essays on the reasons
why some children fail in school.  He argues the following points:

	Compulsory schooling, by its very nature, causes feelings of rebellion
within the child.  Some children purposely fail because it gives them some
sort of control of a situation they're forced to be in.  Imagine the reactions
of the teacher's if they were subject to the same indignities commonly foisted 
onto students.  For example, students are often forced to get the permission 
to go to the bathroom, have no control over the content or speed of learning,
and cannot talk or leave  without permission. 

	Schooling is also a very artificial way to learn.  Holt argues that the
most natural way to learn is by answering questions that come up in the pursuit
of some useful and/or interesting task.  Schoolwork is typically neither
useful nor interesting.  Children are 
presented with answers to questions they never had.  Moreover, they are taught
that if they fail to memorize these answers and spew them back out, they will
be labelled a failure.  Many children turn themselves off when compelled to
learn what is to them both useless and boring.  

	Holt argues a number of other points (against grading, school reform).
He has also written at least 7-8 other books, most of them about education, but
a few about the broader role of children.  He argues that children should be
treated as much like an adult as they are willing to handle.  He opposes
anti-runaway laws, laws restricting children's ability to read/watch so-called
obscene literature, and supports children's right to work.

	His views run counter to extropian views in some areas.  For example
he supports a government subsidized minimum living allowance for children.
On the whole, however, I think he's powerful defender of the freedom of 
children.

	The second book is "Impro" by Keith Johnstone.  The book is a 
collection of improvisational theater games developed by Johnstone while
working as a playwright in London.  Improvisational theater doesn't require
much: no sets, no script, just a few chairs, some actors, and empty space.  
Common to all of the games is the idea of "No Blocking"  

John:  "You made an indecent proposal to my wife!"
Adam:	"No, I didn't"

Adam blocks John's imputation and the scene dies.  In order for improvisation
to work, Adam must accept.

John:  "You made an indecent proposal to my wife!"
Adam:	"Why yes....and she's a very agreeable woman."

	Now the scene can go somewhere.  Johnstone also presents interesting
games for overcoming blocks to creativity, status interactions, and Mask work.
Johnstone also started TheaterSports, a form of acting which incorporates his
ideas.  
        



Chris Rasch
rasch@leland.stanford.edu




