From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Fri, 11 Feb 94 13:25:32 PST
Subject: [#94-2-112] Bounded Random Walks (was Nanarchy)

FutureNerd Steve Witham wrote (back on 3 Feb):
>> Perhaps Fnerd should first summarize the argument(s) why one 
>> person or a small group would take over without blue goo.
>
>... Factor out the overall expansion and the borders are shifting
>along the surface of the sphere.  ... if someone starts out with a
>slightly better method and tradeoff of expansion/defense/research/
>exploiting-territory, then their patch on the sphere surface just
>slowly expands till it's the whole surface, at which point everyone
>else is enclosed.

Let me respond indirectly to this argument, by showing how very
general it is.  Consider any zero-mean stationary random walk within
any bounded subset of Euclidean space (R^n).  That is, a point wanders
around in some finite volume, the average of the places that point
might move to in the next "step" (or short time interval) is the place
where that point is now, and if the point every wanders back to the
same place it would have the same chances of the same moves.

In this case, the probability distribution of where the point goes
must eventually pile up at the boundry of the volume.  So, for
example, pebbles on a walled-in road eventually pile up on the sides
of the road.  Dust collects in the corners of your house.  A stream of
relevant bits of evidence regarding some claim should eventually lead
your beliefs to near certainty for or against that claim.  And for a
fixed set of irrelevant but competing genes, in a finite population of
hosts that randomly live or die, all but one of the competitors will
eventually disappear through "genetic drift".

So if you imagined a fixed set of nations with random border wars,
you'd predict eventually one nation taking over everything.  If you
imagined a fixed set of multinational corporations competing, randomly
doing better or worse in each market, you'd predict that eventually
only one corporation would survive.  If you imagine nations competing
economically, you'd imagine eventually one would have almost all the
GDP.  Similar models might predict convergence to only one language or
religion.

Of course such predictions are rightly suspect.  These models ignore,
for example, processes of generation and fragmentation of the
competing entities.  I claim arguments like Steve's above, predicting
a single "goo" dominating the future, are suspect for quite similar
reasons.

Robin Hanson

