Right.  What it doesn't do is define a basis by which "the whole truth 
*and* nothing but the truth" can be derived.  PCR doesn't guarantee 
that if you follow it well enough, you'll never be wrong.

> >> one reason I don't believe that there are evil demons forcing me
> >> to type this is that I have no evidence for doing so.
> >
> >In this particular example, you could criticize based on uselessness,
> 
> That only invites infinite regress as to the nature and relevance of use-
> fulness, and what would constitute evidence in support of it and evidence
> in opposition to it.

I think it only invites infinite regress if someone's inclined to it.
If a criticism satisfies someone, then that's the end of it for then.
What constitutes evidence is what works to convince oneself or someone else.

> >...the assumption
> >is that you already have beliefs about good sources of information, and 
> >from these sources and your trust in them you get more beliefs.  So as long as
> >you "hold-open-to-criticism" both the trees and their fruits, you're okay.
> 
> Here we have the apparent split personality of PCR: either any use of justifi-
> cationism is bad, or it's okay to use it so long as you do it carefully.  Safe
> Justificationism!  Epistemological prophylaxis!  Don't forget to use your
> mind condom!  Abstinence or caution, that is the question.

The distinction is whether justification is allowed or demanded.  And whether 
it's a good test for truth.  PCR seems to say justification is as allowable
as any other means of *generating* ideas (e.g., tea leaves or indigestion), but 
not a defense for them nor okay to set up as a requirement.  I feel left a
little high and dry about how to get along without justification, but I think
that's the point, that ideas *should* be out there shivering in the cold,
waiting for the axe!  I admire Bartley for being brrracing in his quiet way.

> And Bartley's contempt for defining his terms doesn't help, either.  Which
> is it?  In one sense, his negative case (against traditional forms of
> justification) seems to succeed, but his positive case seems to fail.

I don't know what you mean by "his positive case."  Are you sure there is
one?  Are you sure there needs to be one?

