Subject: [#94-2-117] PCR (Pancritical Rationalism): New book.

My thanks to those who corrected my statement that The Retreat To 
Commitment was out of print.  Apparently it was reprinted sometime in 
the last year.

Along with my copy of RTC, ordered from Open Court, was a catalog of 
new books.  I noticed a forthcoming work by well-known philosophy 
David Miller in the PCR tradition.  Here's the blurb:

The Possibility of Empirical Science
David Miller

Ever since Hume showed that a conclusive justification for generalizing 
or inductive inferences is unattainable, most rationalists have accepted 
this skeptical result, and have retreated to a position that asks only for 
partial justification -- often in terms of judgments of probability.

David Miller maintains that this is a major blunder, since all justification 
demands further justification.  The correct response is to kick the 
justificationist habit completely, and turn back to the idea that what we 
seek is the plain unvarnished truth.  Epistemological concerns about 
how the status of our hypotheses is altered during an investigation thus 
give way to methodological concerns about what techniques will help us 
to sort out truth from error.  No justification can be given, and none is 
needed for such a procedure.

Drawing upon the work of Karl Popper and W.W. Bartley III, Dr. Miller 
argues for an approach to rationality freed frm the debilitating 
authoritarian dependence on reasons and justification.

The currently fashionable Bayesian approach to the philosophy of 
science, in which an interest in truth is replaced entirely by an appeal to 
considerations of probability, is subject to a detailed critical appraisal.  
Probability theory plays only a small role in understanding the growth 
of human knowledge, but objective probabilities do exist in the world.  
The book proposes an intriguing new objectivist interpretation that 
makes sense of objective single-case probabilities even in a deterministic 
universe.

Dr. Miller gives an extremely full discussion of Popper's solution to the 
problem of induction, especially in the form which relates to practical 
decision-making.  All known attempts to impeach Popper's solution as 
skeptical, irrationalist, or implicitly inductivist, are carefully considered 
and refuted.

[Biography ommited.]

ca. 220 pages.  Bibliography. Index.  June.
